WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:07.174
"Why Do Most Users Quit GLP-1 Drugs Like Ozempic
(Semaglutide) Within Months?"

00:00:07.174 --> 00:00:09.376
As the popularity
of Ozempic-type drugs,

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:11.645
like semaglutide, soars,

00:00:11.645 --> 00:00:12.713
what do we need to know

00:00:12.713 --> 00:00:15.082
about their effect
on our weight?

00:00:15.082 --> 00:00:17.618
Even these newfangled
anti-obesity medications

00:00:17.618 --> 00:00:19.553
aren’t a magic bullet.

00:00:19.553 --> 00:00:21.889
The overall weight-loss benefits
are notable,

00:00:21.889 --> 00:00:24.424
but nonetheless limited.

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:28.262
The best of them can produce
a 20 to 25 percent weight loss,

00:00:28.262 --> 00:00:31.531
but they still leave most users
overweight or obese,

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:33.433
only modestly less so,

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.703
and, thus, still facing
major health risks.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:38.906
And, of course,
the drugs are only effective

00:00:38.906 --> 00:00:40.941
as long as they’re used.

00:00:40.941 --> 00:00:42.809
Not only does
the weight loss plateau,

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.444
but years or, presumably,

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:46.613
a lifetime of continuing
treatment is required

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:49.016
just to maintain
their initial benefits,

00:00:49.016 --> 00:00:50.584
and most people
stop taking them

00:00:50.584 --> 00:00:55.289
within months before even
reaching an effective dose.

00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:57.758
The manufacturers
recommend ramping up dosage

00:00:57.758 --> 00:01:00.294
over four or five months,

00:01:00.294 --> 00:01:04.264
but based on 169,000
real-world patients

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:06.667
using GLP-1 drugs
for weight management,

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:10.637
most individuals didn’t stay
on their prescribed treatment

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:12.105
for even three months,

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:13.607
which suggests
it was unlikely

00:01:13.607 --> 00:01:15.876
they were achieving clinically
meaningful weight loss.

00:01:15.876 --> 00:01:19.279
In fact, only about half take
the drug for even two months,

00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:21.915
and 80 percent quit
by six months.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:25.185
Why don’t people stick with it?

00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:28.355
One reason may be
that these drugs are costly.

00:01:28.355 --> 00:01:30.691
And costly is a bit
of an understatement.

00:01:30.691 --> 00:01:32.159
The costs of these drugs
are enormous,

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:35.395
like $1,000 a month
in perpetuity,

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:37.564
since any lost pounds
can pile back on

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:39.032
if you stop taking them.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:42.035
Wegovy, the high-dose Ozempic
used for weight loss,

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:45.305
costs up to about
$1,350 dollars a month.

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:49.843
So, that’s like paying $15,000
out-of-pocket every year.

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:53.046
And some say healthy eating
is expensive?

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:56.049
You could have Food Is Medicine
programs deliver healthy food

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:58.819
to your door for ten times less.

00:01:58.819 --> 00:02:00.921
You could even have
fully prepared meals

00:02:00.921 --> 00:02:03.457
delivered to your door
at a fraction of the cost

00:02:03.457 --> 00:02:05.125
of the drug.

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:07.794
Of course, the drugs
should get cheaper.

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:10.464
There are hopes that competition
will lower prices,

00:02:10.464 --> 00:02:14.534
but each GLP-1 drug is protected
by approximately 20 patents,

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:16.636
many up to 2040 or beyond;

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:20.273
so, they probably won’t get
much cheaper anytime soon.

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:25.379
But isn’t obesity itself
expensive?

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:27.180
Even factoring in
health benefits

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.315
and discounted prices,

00:02:28.315 --> 00:02:31.618
these drugs
may not be cost effective.

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:34.454
In one analysis,
total annual health care costs

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:36.256
doubled for patients,

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:38.325
going from about $13,000
per year

00:02:38.325 --> 00:02:42.729
before starting the drug
to $26,000 after.

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:46.299
Now, the benefits of weight loss
accrue over time,

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:48.969
but because lost pounds
are commonly regained

00:02:48.969 --> 00:02:50.270
after stopping the drug,

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.206
an additional vexing conundrum
is created for payers

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:54.674
like insurance companies.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:57.778
Why shell out all that money
to get people the drug,

00:02:57.778 --> 00:02:59.746
if most are just
gonna stop taking it

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:02.149
and simply regain the weight?

00:03:02.149 --> 00:03:04.918
Why are the drugs so expensive?

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:08.121
"There is no rational reason,
other than greed,"

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:09.990
according to Senator Sanders,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.292
noting that Canadians
and Germans

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:15.829
are paying about 10 times less
for the same drugs.

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:18.198
Wegovy, the high-dose Ozempic
used for weight loss,

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.801
can sell for $1,300 a month
in the U.S.

00:03:20.801 --> 00:03:24.905
but less than a hundred
a month in the U.K.

00:03:24.905 --> 00:03:26.740
Here’s what
different countries pay

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.242
for the exact same dose
of Ozempic.

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:33.113
The researchers even calculated
the "estimated minimum price,"

00:03:33.113 --> 00:03:35.282
how much it could
be manufactured

00:03:35.282 --> 00:03:37.417
and sold profitably for.

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:39.820
So, for 40 dollars a month,

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.922
Novo Nordisk,
the makers of Ozempic,

00:03:41.922 --> 00:03:44.524
could be pulling in
a 10 percent profit margin.

00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:46.426
But why make 10 percent profit

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:50.097
when you can make
more than 3,000% profit?

00:03:50.097 --> 00:03:52.532
But don’t they need
those huge profit margins

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.568
to recoup research
and development?

00:03:54.568 --> 00:03:57.771
I mean, it’s expensive
to develop drugs.

00:03:57.771 --> 00:04:00.774
Novo Nordisk spends
twice as much money

00:04:00.774 --> 00:04:02.742
enriching its shareholders,

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:05.045
by doing things
like stock buybacks

00:04:05.045 --> 00:04:08.949
to jack up stock prices,
than it does on all R&amp;D.

00:04:08.949 --> 00:04:11.284
In fact,
most big drug companies

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:14.421
spend more on these
kind of self-enrichment schemes

00:04:14.421 --> 00:04:16.523
than actual research.

00:04:16.523 --> 00:04:18.959
Whatever the actual cost
of developing a drug,

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:23.096
drug companies may spend
far more on marketing drugs

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:25.665
than developing them.

00:04:25.665 --> 00:04:28.568
For example, while gouging
patients $1,000 a month

00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:29.469
for their drugs,

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:31.872
Novo Nordisk was spending
millions of dollars

00:04:31.872 --> 00:04:35.876
wining and dining doctors
to prescribe more of them.

00:04:35.876 --> 00:04:37.844
Hundreds of thousands
of meals and snacks

00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:40.480
served to promote drugs
like Ozempic.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.649
In fact, Novo Nordisk
was actually suspended

00:04:42.649 --> 00:04:45.051
from a pharmaceutical industry
trade association

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:47.254
for failing to disclose
its sponsorship

00:04:47.254 --> 00:04:49.422
of a training course
for healthcare professionals

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:53.193
that just so happened
to plug one of its GLP-1 drugs,

00:04:53.193 --> 00:04:54.261
a violation bad enough

00:04:54.261 --> 00:04:55.762
for the Royal College
of Physicians

00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:59.833
to sever its ties
with the drug giant.

00:04:59.833 --> 00:05:01.668
Anyway, back to
the original question:

00:05:01.668 --> 00:05:04.504
Why do so few people
stay on these drugs?

00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:07.474
Well, people may start out
obese and end up obese.

00:05:07.474 --> 00:05:11.478
Is it worth paying $1,000
a month just to stay less obese?

00:05:11.478 --> 00:05:14.514
You can see why people
might just cut their losses.

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:18.785
But no, the study showed that
most people who quit these drugs

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:22.622
after just a few months
were not only insured,

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:26.092
they had their drugs
covered by their insurance.

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:28.328
So, maybe the reason
most people quit

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:31.431
so soon after starting
is not due to their cost,

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:34.601
but rather their side effects,

00:05:34.601 --> 00:05:37.470
which we’ll cover next.

